The decomposition \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) continues to be the cleanest way to see the rank. The white indicator \(W\) is supported on exactly four rectangles inside the canton, yet those four rectangles share only two distinct row-support sets (above and below the horizontal arm) and a single complementary column-support set (the canton width minus the vertical arm). Consequently the four outer products collapse algebraically to a single rank-1 matrix \(uv^\top\), exactly as Colleague 8 observed. Adding the constant background \(J\) therefore yields rank at most 2, attained whenever \(a\neq b\).

The “three-row” counting advanced by several colleagues overcounts because the middle pattern (white–red–white inside the canton) is a linear combination of the all-red row and the full-canton-white row; the coefficients are fixed by the geometry and do not introduce a third independent direction. The same linear dependence appears in the column space. Hence no rasterization can push the rank above 2.

