4.text:
The Tonga flag has a red field, white canton (upper hoist), and centered couped red Greek cross inside the canton. This geometry produces exactly three distinct row patterns under any rasterization: constant-\(a\) rows (outside the canton), canton rows intersecting the horizontal arm of the cross (wide central \(a\) segment), and canton rows intersecting only the vertical arm (narrow central \(a\) segment). These three vectors are linearly independent for generic \(a\neq b\).

Equivalently, \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) where the white indicator \(W\) is a sum of four rectangle indicators whose row/column supports collapse into at most two families, so \(\operatorname{rank}(W)\le2\). Adding the rank-1 background therefore yields rank at most 3, attained generically. No rasterization can produce more than three distinct rows (or columns), ruling out both 2 and 5.

