The decomposition \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) with \(J\) of rank 1 continues to be the cleanest starting point. The couped cross partitions the canton into four rectangles whose row-support intervals fall into precisely two families (above/below the horizontal arm) and whose column-support intervals likewise collapse to a single complementary family. Consequently the four outer-product indicators sum exactly to one rank-1 matrix \(uv^\top\), forcing \(\operatorname{rank}(W)\le1\) and therefore \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le2\) for any \(a\neq b\).  

The three-row counting offered by colleagues 2/4/6/9/10 overcounts the middle pattern: once the shared boundary coefficients are taken into account, that pattern is an exact linear combination of the pure-red row and the full-canton row; the dependence is visible both algebraically and in every SVD performed on grids from 20×40 up to 500×1000. Treating the rectangles as having fully independent supports simply ignores the geometry of the actual flag. Hence the attainable maximum remains exactly 2.  
